From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 21:43:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] BD-J: an animation that does not pause is shown at least
 every three refresh ticks

The graphics plane of a BD-J title is sent to the player by a timer that
ticks every 40 ms once something changed, and refreshes only at a tick
with no change since the tick before. A menu that animates without a
pause changes at every tick: none of its frames was shown until the
animation stopped, and the menu looked frozen or appeared late.

A refresh that has waited 100 ms (from the change that started it) is
now made at its next tick whatever changed since, with what the menu has
drawn by then: an animation is shown about every 130 ms. A window that
changes less often than the timer ticks, a single change and a short
burst of changes are refreshed exactly as before, once nothing changed
for a tick. The decision is refreshAtTick(); the time is the monotonic
clock of the JVM.

Checked with a host test of the class (the decision of the jar, on a
simulated timeline): a window that changes every 10 ms for 2 s gets 8
refreshes a second (before: none until it stopped), never two within
40 ms, the last 50 ms after its last change; every 50 ms, one change,
and a burst of 60 ms as before (6 checks; two mutations each fail).

The bound of a refresh's wait comes from avdvplus (its bounded sync
deferral), as CroqueMr's libreelec-x86-DV took it; written anew here.
---
 .../bdj/java/java/awt/BDRootWindow.java        | 18 +++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/libbluray/bdj/java/java/awt/BDRootWindow.java b/src/libbluray/bdj/java/java/awt/BDRootWindow.java
index 91b86c7..6e38760 100644
--- a/src/libbluray/bdj/java/java/awt/BDRootWindow.java
+++ b/src/libbluray/bdj/java/java/awt/BDRootWindow.java
@@ -146,15 +146,30 @@ public class BDRootWindow extends Frame {
         }
     }
 
+    /*
+     * Hades: the longest a refresh waits for the window to stop changing. A refresh is made at the
+     * first tick of its timer (every 40 ms) at which nothing changed since the tick before; a menu that
+     * animates without a pause changes at every tick, and its frames were never shown until the
+     * animation stopped. So a refresh that has waited this long is made at its next tick, and an
+     * animation is shown at least about every 120 ms (three ticks), with what it has drawn by then.
+     */
+    static final long MAX_REFRESH_WAIT_MS = 100;
+
+    /* Hades: whether a pending refresh is made at a tick of its timer (RefreshTimerTask) */
+    static boolean refreshAtTick(int changeCountAtLastTick, int changeCount, long waitedMs) {
+        return changeCountAtLastTick == changeCount || waitedMs >= MAX_REFRESH_WAIT_MS;
+    }
+
     private static class RefreshTimerTask extends TimerTask {
         public RefreshTimerTask(BDRootWindow window) {
             this.window = window;
             this.changeCount = window.changeCount;
+            this.since = System.nanoTime();
         }
 
         public void run() {
             synchronized (window) {
-                if (this.changeCount == window.changeCount)
+                if (refreshAtTick(this.changeCount, window.changeCount, (System.nanoTime() - since) / 1000000L))
                     window.sync();
                 else
                     this.changeCount = window.changeCount;
@@ -163,6 +178,7 @@ public class BDRootWindow extends Frame {
 
         private BDRootWindow window;
         private int changeCount;
+        private final long since; /* when the first change of this refresh came (System.nanoTime()) */
     }
 
     private void close() {
